      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Subcutaneous administrations of naloxone and naltrexone have already been shown to enhance nociceptive reactions in mice. The present study was undertaken to examine the effects of N-methyl-naloxone and N-methylnaltrexone on nociception using the hot plate test (dose range: 0.3 to 30 mg kg-1s.c.). The latter compounds were selected to differentiate the central and peripheral components of hyperalgesia. Unlike naloxone, N-methyl-naloxone did not produce hyperalgesia. Similarly low doses of N-methylnaltrexone did not enhance the jumping response. However, a high dose of N-methylnaltrexone (30 mg kg-1 s.c.) significantly reduced the jumping latencies 2 h after its administration. This phenomenon indicated that it might be converted to an active metabolite. Further, N-methylnaloxone and N-methylnaltrexone were very weak in precipitating the signs of abstinence in mice rendered acutely dependent on morphine. Two factors, poorer penetration into the CNS and steric hindrance, might render the N-methylated antagonists weak. Hence, both these factors should be considered when interpreting the effects after quaternary derivatives of opioid antagonists."},
